  "query_preview": "..(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 405 AM EDT THU AUG 20 2026\n\nAt the onset of the long term period, a high amplitude upper \nlevel pattern will be in place, with deep troughing over the \neastern CONUS and strong ridging over the western CONUS. At the \nsurface, a weak high will sit to the northwest of the forecast \narea; meanwhile, an area of low pressure will be located near Lake\nMichigan, with a cold front draped to its southwest. By Friday \nnight, showers and storms will be exiting the forecast area as \nprevious shortwave forcing departs. Overnight, skies will clear \nsomewhat, allowing for temperatures to cool into the low to mid \n60s, with widespread fog developing.\n\nBy Saturday afternoon, another shortwave will move through the \neastern CONUS trough, which will push the aforementioned cold \nfront southeast into the forecast area. In theory, this could \nbring another round of showers and storms, although model spread \nremains quite high at this time. With Eastern Kentucky sitting in \nclose proximity to the upper level trough axis on Saturday, any \ndiffering speed in the motion of this trough could have \nimplications for PoPs. Accordingly, NBM output keeps rain chances \nisolated for much of the forecast area to account for this \nuncertainty. Following the passage of this front, height rises are\nanticipated as the western ridge continues to progress eastward \nand the trough becomes less amplified. \n\nFrom Sunday through early next week, ridging will dominate the \nforecast. Behind Saturday's cold front, a much drier air mass \nwill be in place. Northerly to northeasterly winds will maintain \ndewpoints in the low 60s to even upper 50s, with rain chances near\nzero. High temperatures will range from the low to mid 80s across\nthe forecast area. Overnight, lows will fall into the upper 50s \nin many valley locations and the low 60s elsewhere, with typical \nfog formation expected. This tranquil weather will continue until \nmidweek, when an additional disturbance looks to approach the \narea.",
